<h2>The structure of the rule</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Abandonment without liberty</h3>
    <p>Available at any time, as of right. Consequences: the plaintiff pays such costs as the court awards and is precluded from instituting any fresh suit in respect of the same subject matter or part abandoned.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">vs</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Withdrawal with liberty</h3>
    <p>Granted only where the court is satisfied the suit must fail by reason of a formal defect, or there are sufficient grounds for allowing a fresh suit. The claim survives, subject to limitation and any terms the court imposes.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The policy is anti-harassment: a defendant dragged to court is entitled to finality, and a plaintiff cannot abandon when the evidence turns bad and start again with lessons learned. The liberty gateway exists for cases where the dismissal threatening the suit says nothing about the claim's merits — and it is confined to those cases.</p>

<h2>Applying for liberty: how to do it properly</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Identify the defect precisely.</strong> The application must name the formal defect or sufficient ground — "the plaintiff does not wish to pursue the present suit" earns withdrawal, not liberty.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Apply before the merits are decided.</strong> Liberty sought after adverse findings, or on the eve of judgment, is viewed as an attempt to escape the result; timing is credibility.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Define the subject matter.</strong> The preclusion and the liberty both operate on "subject matter" — the application and the order should describe it so the scope of what may be refiled is not litigated later.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Accept terms.</strong> Costs to the defendant and conditions (often a timeline for the fresh suit) are the ordinary price; build them into the plan rather than resisting them into a refusal.</div>
</div>

<h2>Three traps that recur</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>The limitation illusion</h3><p>Liberty keeps the claim alive procedurally; it does not refresh limitation. Compute limitation for the fresh suit before withdrawing the first one, and assess whether Section 14 exclusion is realistically available.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Partial abandonment by inattention</h3><p>Abandoning "part of a claim" carries the same preclusion for that part. Amendments that drop reliefs, or statements at the bar recorded in orders, can operate as abandonment — audit every deletion against the rule.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The pending counterclaim</h3><p>Withdrawal ends the plaintiff's suit, not the defendant's counterclaim. A plaintiff withdrawing to defuse litigation may find the real battle — now one-sided — continues on the counterclaim.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Withdrawal and settlement</h2>
<p>Where withdrawal implements a settlement, the cleaner instrument is usually a compromise under Order XXIII Rule 3, which records the terms and yields a decree capable of execution. Withdrawal simpliciter against a promise recorded nowhere leaves the plaintiff with the preclusion and only a fresh contractual claim if the promise is broken. Where parties want the suit to end but the settlement to be enforceable, they should say so in the compromise application — and where only some defendants settle, the drafting must preserve the suit against the rest.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Summary: withdraw with liberty only on a genuinely formal defect, before the merits sour, with the subject matter defined and limitation computed; treat every abandonment, however partial, as permanent; and never use withdrawal as the vehicle for a settlement that deserves a decree.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
